<LessonLayout title="The Paschal Mystery in the Sacraments" cic={lessonRef} scripture={lessonScripture}>

<section class="content">

<h2>Big Question</h2>

<p class="big-question">What does a lamb roasted three thousand years ago have to do with your life today?</p>

<p>
Imagine a night. Entire families gathered around a table, sandals on, staff in hand, eating hurriedly. Unleavened bread. Bitter herbs. Meat roasted over fire. Outside, Egypt weeps for its firstborn. Inside, the people of Israel eat their last supper in the land of slavery. That night, the blood of the lamb on the doorposts saves them from death. It is the Passover. And the next day they walk free toward the promised land. Now travel in time: you are in a church, an ordinary Sunday. A priest breaks the bread. He says: <em>&laquo;Behold the Lamb of God.&raquo;</em> The same blood saves, the same freedom is offered. The Paschal Mystery of Christ &mdash;his passing from death to life&mdash; is the key that unlocks all the sacraments. Every sacrament is an extension of that Passover night.
</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>The Sacraments: Works of Christ in the Church</h2>

<p>
The sacraments were instituted by Christ and entrusted to the Church. They are sensible and efficacious signs of grace, through which divine life is communicated to us (CCC 1131). They are not empty rites nor human traditions. They are actions of Christ himself, who continues his saving work through the Church.
</p>

<p>
The Catechism calls them <em>&laquo;forces that flow from the Body of Christ, which is ever-living and life-giving&raquo;</em> (CCC 1116). They are like channels through which the grace of the Paschal Mystery reaches every moment of our lives: when we are born to faith (Baptism), when we grow (Confirmation), when we are nourished (Eucharist), when we fall and get up (Penance), when we suffer (Anointing), when we serve (Holy Orders), when we love (Matrimony).
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--semilla">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Activity</h4>
<p>Make a list of the seven sacraments. Next to each one, write a moment in Jesus' life that relates to it (e.g., Baptism with Jesus' baptism in the Jordan). Do you see how each sacrament touches an aspect of Christ's life?</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>The Paschal Mystery in Each Sacrament</h2>

<p>
The word <em>&laquo;Passover&raquo;</em> or <em>&laquo;Pasch&raquo;</em> means <em>&laquo;passing over&raquo;</em>: the passage from slavery to freedom, from death to life. Christ accomplished this passage perfectly: he passed from the death of Good Friday to the resurrection of Easter Sunday. And that same passage is applied in each sacrament (CCC 1139-1140).
</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Baptism:</strong> we die to sin and rise to new life (Rom 6:3-11).</li>
<li><strong>Confirmation:</strong> we receive the power of the Spirit to live as witnesses of the resurrection.</li>
<li><strong>Eucharist:</strong> we share in the Body and Blood of Christ, the memorial of his Passover.</li>
<li><strong>Penance:</strong> we die to sin and are restored to friendship with God.</li>
<li><strong>Anointing of the Sick:</strong> Christ journeys with the sick in their suffering, giving strength and hope.</li>
<li><strong>Holy Orders:</strong> Christ continues his ministry as head of the Church.</li>
<li><strong>Matrimony:</strong> Christ's love for the Church becomes visible in the self-giving of spouses.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Each sacrament is a paschal encounter: it plunges us into Christ's death to make us sharers in his resurrection (CCC 1139).
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--brotes">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Connection</h4>
<p>Read Romans 6:3-11. Paul says that in Baptism we <em>&laquo;were buried with Christ.&raquo;</em> The word <em>&laquo;buried&raquo;</em> is strong and real. It is not a symbol. It is a real participation in Christ's death. How does your understanding of Baptism change knowing it is not just <em>&laquo;entering the Church&raquo;</em> but dying and rising with Christ?</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Sign and Grace: How the Sacraments Work</h2>

<p>
The sacraments work <em>ex opere operato</em>, that is, by the very fact of the action being performed (CCC 1128). This means that the efficacy of the sacrament does not depend on the holiness of the minister or the disposition of the recipient &mdash;although the recipient's disposition is important for fruitfulness&mdash;. It depends on Christ, who acts through the sacrament.
</p>

<p>
For example, a baptized infant receives sanctifying grace even though he does not understand what is happening. A repentant sinner receives forgiveness in Confession even if the priest is unworthy. This gives us immense security: the sacraments are works of Christ, not human works (CCC 1127).
</p>

<p>
But the grace of the sacrament is not magic. It requires faith. The disposition of the recipient &mdash;faith, repentance, desire&mdash; determines the fruit the sacrament produces. As St. Augustine says: <em>&laquo;God who created you without you, will not save you without you&raquo;</em> (CCC 1129).
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--raiz">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Context</h4>
<p><em>Ex opere operato</em> means <em>&laquo;by the work performed.&raquo;</em> This doctrine was defined at the Council of Trent to defend that the sacraments are true channels of grace, not mere symbols. Why is it important to know that the efficacy of the sacrament does not depend on the priest? What consolation does this give you?</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Sacramental Character: An Indelible Mark</h2>

<p>
Three sacraments &mdash;Baptism, Confirmation, and Holy Orders&mdash; imprint a spiritual, indelible character on the soul (CCC 1121). This means they cannot be repeated. Once you are baptized, you are a child of God forever. Once confirmed, you are a witness of Christ forever. Once ordained, you are a priest forever.
</p>

<p>
This character is like a mark of God's ownership. Paul states it powerfully: <em>&laquo;You are not your own, for you were bought with a price&raquo;</em> (1 Cor 6:19-20). That mark configures us to Christ permanently. It gives us an identity that nothing and no one can erase (CCC 1272-1274).
</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--arbol">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">Going Deeper</h4>
<p>The sacramental character configures the Christian to Christ in three ways: as child (Baptism), as witness (Confirmation), and as servant (Holy Orders). Each one is a participation in the priesthood of Christ. How do you live your baptismal character today? Are you aware that you bear an eternal mark of God?</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Biblical Connection: The Passover and Our Passover</h2>

<blockquote>
<p><em>&laquo;The Lord said to Moses and Aaron in the land of Egypt: 'This month shall be for you the beginning of months&hellip; On the tenth day of this month, let every one take a lamb&hellip; You shall keep it until the fourteenth day of this month, when the whole assembly of the congregation of Israel shall kill their lambs at twilight. They shall take some of the blood and put it on the two doorposts and the lintel of the houses in which they eat it&hellip; They shall eat the flesh that night, roasted on the fire, with unleavened bread and bitter herbs&hellip; It is the Lord's Passover.'&raquo;</em></p>
<cite>Exodus 12:1-11</cite>
</blockquote>

<p>
Paul interprets this in light of Christ: <em>&laquo;Christ, our Passover lamb, has been sacrificed. Let us therefore celebrate the festival, not with the old leaven, the leaven of malice and evil, but with the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth&raquo;</em> (1 Cor 5:7-8). What Israel lived as liberation from Egypt, we live as liberation from sin through the blood of Christ. The same paschal logic runs through all of salvation history (CCC 1151).
</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>For Reflection</h2>

<ol>
<li>Which is your favorite sacrament and why? How have you experienced the Paschal Mystery in it?</li>
<li>The Catechism says the sacraments are <em>&laquo;forces that flow from the Body of Christ.&raquo;</em> What does this mean for your spiritual life?</li>
<li>Knowing that the sacraments work <em>ex opere operato</em>, does it give you security or challenge you to have a better disposition?</li>
<li>How can you live this week more aware of your Baptism and Confirmation?</li>
<li>Of the seven sacraments, which one do you think you need most at this moment in your life?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>

<div class="callout">
<p><strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> The Paschal Mystery of Christ &mdash;his death and resurrection&mdash; is made present and active in every sacrament, bringing us from death to life.</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Cultural Resources</h2>

<p>
<strong><em>&laquo;The Adoration of the Mystic Lamb&raquo; by Jan van Eyck</em></strong> &mdash; Also known as the Ghent Altarpiece, this 15th-century masterpiece depicts the adoration of the Lamb of God at the center, from whom a river of living water flows. It is an image of the grace that flows from Christ in the sacraments. The wounded yet victorious lamb is Christ, whose blood purifies us and gives us life.
</p>

<p>
<strong>St. John of the Cross: <em>&laquo;The Dark Night&raquo;</em></strong> &mdash; In his poem, St. John describes the soul's passage toward God as a nocturnal crossing. The sacraments are like the pillars of that bridge: they sustain us in the darkness, keeping us united to Christ when we cannot see the path. They are the anchor in the storm.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Music: <em>&laquo;Victimae Paschali Laudes&raquo;</em></strong> &mdash; This Easter sequence from the 11th century is sung at the Easter Mass. Its refrain summarizes the Paschal Mystery: <em>&laquo;Death and life contended in a stupendous duel: the Author of life, dead, lives and reigns.&raquo;</em> Every sacrament participates in this stupendous duel between death and life, sin and grace.
</p>

</section>

